passages.text:
In the spring of 1860, a letter sent from Missouri to California by stagecoach usually took three weeks or more to arrive. A new company promised to deliver mail in about ten days, saving customers more than a {{1|weeks}} wait. Its service soon became known as the Pony Express.

The plan depended on speed at every step. The company set up a chain of relay stations across roughly 1,900 miles of prairie, mountains, and desert. Each rider, along with the mail he carried, {{2|were}} expected to cover about seventy-five to one hundred miles before handing off to a fresh rider. At each station along the way, he swapped his tired horse for a fresh {{3|one, and}} rode on. Station keepers had the next horse saddled and waiting, so each change took {{4|some time}}. Riders needed to be both brave and {{5|have a light weight}}, since every extra pound slowed the horses.

The mail itself traveled in a leather cover called a mochila, which fit over the saddle and could be moved from one horse to the next in seconds. Because of this system, the service was {{6|capable to}} remarkable feats. In March 1861, riders carried the text of Abraham Lincoln's first inaugural address between the ends of the telegraph lines in Nebraska and Nevada in under eight days. {{7|}} Despite such feats, the company was losing money. Postage was expensive, at first five dollars for every half ounce of mail, but the cost of the stations, horses, and riders was far greater. {{8|}}

In October 1861, workers completed the first transcontinental telegraph line, which could carry a message across the country almost instantly. Two days later, the Pony Express {{9|announces}} that it would close. {{10|}}
